1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,366
Dawn, June 25th, 1950. Rain was falling

2
00:00:04,386 --> 00:00:06,756
on Chuncheon, Gangwon Province.

3
00:00:06,925 --> 00:00:08,828
From beyond the 38th Parallel,

4
00:00:08,848 --> 00:00:11,794
North Korean shells began to rain down.

5
00:00:12,227 --> 00:00:14,522
The North Korean plan was bold.

6
00:00:14,648 --> 00:00:17,154
Take Chuncheon on day one, then swing

7
00:00:17,174 --> 00:00:20,141
southwest and encircle Seoul from behind.

8
00:00:20,587 --> 00:00:23,402
If it worked, the main South Korean army in

9
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:26,110
Seoul would be trapped within three days.

10
00:00:26,545 --> 00:00:27,925
But the plan began to fall

11
00:00:27,945 --> 00:00:29,927
apart on the very first day.

12
00:00:30,004 --> 00:00:32,272
At Chuncheon, a single division stood in

13
00:00:32,292 --> 00:00:35,237
the way of an entire North Korean corps.

14
00:00:35,813 --> 00:00:38,119
On the first day of the war, as the front

15
00:00:38,139 --> 00:00:41,090
collapsed all along the 38th Parallel,

16
00:00:41,213 --> 00:00:43,937
only one place held: Chuncheon.

17
00:00:44,382 --> 00:00:45,773
An underdog defense that

18
00:00:45,793 --> 00:00:47,844
bought three precious days.

19
00:00:47,901 --> 00:00:49,292
This is the story of the Battle

20
00:00:49,312 --> 00:00:50,954
of Chuncheon, the fight that

21
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:53,482
wrecked the plan to encircle Seoul.

22
00:00:53,919 --> 00:00:55,954
In June 1950, the 38th

23
00:00:55,974 --> 00:00:58,390
Parallel was strangely quiet.

24
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:00,611
With the weekend coming, many South Korean

25
00:01:00,631 --> 00:01:03,657
units let their men go on pass and leave.

26
00:01:04,082 --> 00:01:05,709
The central front around Chuncheon and

27
00:01:05,729 --> 00:01:09,521
Hongcheon belonged to the ROK 6th Division.

28
00:01:09,647 --> 00:01:11,540
The 7th Regiment held Chuncheon,

29
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:13,702
the 2nd held Hongcheon, and the

30
00:01:13,722 --> 00:01:16,496
19th waited in reserve at Wonju.

31
00:01:16,935 --> 00:01:19,219
Its commander, Colonel Kim Jong-oh,

32
00:01:19,239 --> 00:01:21,665
had been in charge for barely two weeks.

33
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:24,182
From captured infiltrators and defectors,

34
00:01:24,202 --> 00:01:26,683
he sensed that something was wrong.

35
00:01:27,124 --> 00:01:28,803
Two years later, he would lead the

36
00:01:28,823 --> 00:01:30,625
9th Division at White Horse Hill

37
00:01:30,645 --> 00:01:33,514
against China's elite 38th Army.

38
00:01:33,564 --> 00:01:34,944
His first real test came

39
00:01:34,964 --> 00:01:36,796
right here, at Chuncheon.

40
00:01:37,209 --> 00:01:39,239
Kim cancelled passes and overnight

41
00:01:39,259 --> 00:01:41,458
leave, and put his men on alert.

42
00:01:41,578 --> 00:01:43,618
Trenches were re-dug, and his artillery

43
00:01:43,638 --> 00:01:45,444
was pulled in toward the town.

44
00:01:45,883 --> 00:01:47,231
Chuncheon sits in a basin

45
00:01:47,251 --> 00:01:49,211
wrapped by the Soyang River.

46
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:51,726
North of the river lay the open fields of

47
00:01:51,746 --> 00:01:55,312
Oksan-po; south of it, Bongui Mountain,

48
00:01:55,332 --> 00:01:59,006
301 meters high, looked down over the town.

49
00:01:59,442 --> 00:02:01,900
To reach Chuncheon, an attacker had to cross

50
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:05,222
open fields and then the Soyang River.

51
00:02:05,313 --> 00:02:07,045
And every step of that route could be

52
00:02:07,065 --> 00:02:09,313
seen from the top of Bongui Mountain.

53
00:02:09,736 --> 00:02:12,464
Facing them was the North Korean II Corps.

54
00:02:12,603 --> 00:02:14,756
Its 2nd Division aimed at Chuncheon,

55
00:02:14,776 --> 00:02:17,272
its 7th Division at Hongcheon,

56
00:02:17,292 --> 00:02:20,687
led by Soviet-built self-propelled guns.

57
00:02:21,101 --> 00:02:23,575
Under the North Korean plan, II Corps

58
00:02:23,595 --> 00:02:26,170
was to take Chuncheon on the first day.

59
00:02:26,266 --> 00:02:28,317
Then it would drive through Hongcheon and

60
00:02:28,337 --> 00:02:32,429
Icheon to Suwon, cutting in behind Seoul.

61
00:02:32,857 --> 00:02:34,748
Dawn, June 25th.

62
00:02:34,845 --> 00:02:37,477
After a thirty-minute barrage, North Korean

63
00:02:37,497 --> 00:02:40,468
troops poured across the 38th Parallel.

64
00:02:40,904 --> 00:02:42,918
Breaking through the outposts, the North

65
00:02:42,938 --> 00:02:45,753
Koreans rolled onto the Oksan-po fields

66
00:02:45,773 --> 00:02:48,676
behind some ten self-propelled guns.

67
00:02:48,776 --> 00:02:50,441
The South Korean soldiers had never

68
00:02:50,461 --> 00:02:53,167
seen armored monsters like these.

69
00:02:53,575 --> 00:02:55,718
Shells fell nonstop among the

70
00:02:55,738 --> 00:02:57,698
rain-soaked rice paddies.

71
00:02:57,764 --> 00:02:59,861
The 1st Battalion, 7th Regiment,

72
00:02:59,881 --> 00:03:02,661
guarding Oksan-po, faced that

73
00:03:02,681 --> 00:03:04,918
armored column with almost nothing.

74
00:03:05,332 --> 00:03:07,115
The South Korean army had no tanks

75
00:03:07,135 --> 00:03:09,653
and no real anti-tank weapons.

76
00:03:09,776 --> 00:03:12,613
All it had were 57mm anti-tank

77
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:15,011
guns and a few bazookas.

78
00:03:15,442 --> 00:03:16,277
Then the gunners at

79
00:03:16,297 --> 00:03:18,223
Chuncheon made a bold call.

80
00:03:18,312 --> 00:03:19,453
They lowered the barrels of

81
00:03:19,473 --> 00:03:22,446
their 105mm howitzers and fired

82
00:03:22,466 --> 00:03:24,530
straight at the oncoming guns.

83
00:03:24,979 --> 00:03:26,123
A howitzer is meant to

84
00:03:26,143 --> 00:03:28,149
lob shells over hills.

85
00:03:28,203 --> 00:03:30,038
They turned it into a gun aimed

86
00:03:30,058 --> 00:03:32,156
at armor right in front of them.

87
00:03:32,582 --> 00:03:34,051
And in the middle of the fields,

88
00:03:34,071 --> 00:03:36,258
a young officer moved forward.

89
00:03:36,376 --> 00:03:38,474
He was Second Lieutenant Shim Il,

90
00:03:38,494 --> 00:03:41,926
an anti-tank platoon leader of the 7th Regiment.

91
00:03:42,353 --> 00:03:44,276
When shells failed, he and his

92
00:03:44,296 --> 00:03:46,029
men crawled toward the guns with

93
00:03:46,049 --> 00:03:48,646
grenades and Molotov cocktails.

94
00:03:48,776 --> 00:03:50,349
They reportedly got close enough to

95
00:03:50,369 --> 00:03:52,558
drop grenades through the hatches.

96
00:03:52,986 --> 00:03:55,132
According to official records, his platoon

97
00:03:55,152 --> 00:03:58,091
stopped several of the self-propelled guns.

98
00:03:58,207 --> 00:03:59,974
Word that the armored monsters could be

99
00:03:59,994 --> 00:04:02,902
destroyed spread quickly along the front.

100
00:04:03,332 --> 00:04:04,216
His feat was later

101
00:04:04,236 --> 00:04:06,003
questioned as exaggerated,

102
00:04:06,057 --> 00:04:09,099
but in 2017 a Ministry of National Defense

103
00:04:09,119 --> 00:04:11,590
review panel concluded it was true.

104
00:04:12,032 --> 00:04:14,167
On June 26th, the North Koreans

105
00:04:14,187 --> 00:04:16,236
tried to cross the Soyang River.

106
00:04:16,683 --> 00:04:18,374
From the top of Bongui Mountain,

107
00:04:18,394 --> 00:04:21,048
observers called every North Korean move along

108
00:04:21,068 --> 00:04:23,483
the riverbank down to the artillery.

109
00:04:23,599 --> 00:04:25,457
Shells landed exactly where

110
00:04:25,477 --> 00:04:27,196
the enemy tried to cross.

111
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:29,823
Under South Korean fire from the heights,

112
00:04:29,843 --> 00:04:32,008
the river became a death trap.

113
00:04:32,094 --> 00:04:33,599
Reportedly, only about one

114
00:04:33,619 --> 00:04:36,068
company actually made it across.

115
00:04:36,487 --> 00:04:38,709
Remarkably, even a North Korean novel

116
00:04:38,729 --> 00:04:41,042
portrays this moment as a defeat.

117
00:04:41,142 --> 00:04:42,978
It says a battalion collapsed within

118
00:04:42,998 --> 00:04:46,604
minutes under a storm of unseen bullets.

119
00:04:47,016 --> 00:04:49,739
Meanwhile at Hongcheon, the 2nd Regiment was

120
00:04:49,759 --> 00:04:52,538
holding back the North Korean 7th Division.

121
00:04:52,703 --> 00:04:55,711
On the narrow Malgogae pass, North Korean

122
00:04:55,731 --> 00:04:58,908
tanks ground to a halt one by one.

123
00:04:59,347 --> 00:05:00,909
Stalled at Chuncheon, the North

124
00:05:00,929 --> 00:05:02,798
Koreans changed their plan.

125
00:05:02,887 --> 00:05:05,030
They diverted troops heading toward Inje

126
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:07,885
and poured their firepower onto Chuncheon.

127
00:05:08,335 --> 00:05:10,389
On June 27th, the North Koreans

128
00:05:10,409 --> 00:05:12,505
finally crossed the Soyang River.

129
00:05:12,594 --> 00:05:14,144
The 7th Regiment fell back to

130
00:05:14,164 --> 00:05:16,557
the Wonchang Pass south of town

131
00:05:16,577 --> 00:05:18,674
and blocked the road again.

132
00:05:19,099 --> 00:05:20,256
But by then, the western

133
00:05:20,276 --> 00:05:21,940
front had already collapsed.

134
00:05:21,995 --> 00:05:24,547
On June 28th, Seoul fell.

135
00:05:24,978 --> 00:05:26,738
After taking Seoul, the North Korean

136
00:05:26,758 --> 00:05:29,106
army halted there for three days.

137
00:05:29,195 --> 00:05:31,131
Some historians argue it was because

138
00:05:31,151 --> 00:05:32,646
II Corps, which was meant to

139
00:05:32,666 --> 00:05:35,734
close the trap, had not yet arrived.

140
00:05:36,186 --> 00:05:38,418
To avoid being cut off, the 6th

141
00:05:38,438 --> 00:05:40,375
Division now had to pull back.

142
00:05:40,449 --> 00:05:42,023
It did not collapse; it

143
00:05:42,043 --> 00:05:44,245
withdrew south in good order.

144
00:05:44,677 --> 00:05:47,674
Even after losing Hongcheon on June 30th,

145
00:05:47,695 --> 00:05:49,576
the 6th Division fought its way back

146
00:05:49,596 --> 00:05:52,023
toward Wonju and Chungju, and kept

147
00:05:52,043 --> 00:05:54,109
itself together as a fighting unit.

148
00:05:54,526 --> 00:05:55,724
So how did one division

149
00:05:55,744 --> 00:05:57,590
hold up an entire corps?

150
00:05:57,696 --> 00:05:59,777
Let's break it down into four factors.

151
00:06:00,196 --> 00:06:02,759
First, they were ready in advance.

152
00:06:03,176 --> 00:06:04,777
One decision to cancel passes

153
00:06:04,797 --> 00:06:06,473
meant the 6th Division's men were

154
00:06:06,493 --> 00:06:08,259
at their posts that morning.

155
00:06:08,406 --> 00:06:10,821
Many other units were caught half-empty.

156
00:06:11,249 --> 00:06:13,664
Second, the river and the mountain.

157
00:06:14,098 --> 00:06:16,301
Open fields, the Soyang River,

158
00:06:16,321 --> 00:06:17,745
and Bongui Mountain.

159
00:06:17,834 --> 00:06:19,612
The attackers had nowhere to hide,

160
00:06:19,632 --> 00:06:21,819
while the defenders could see everything.

161
00:06:22,249 --> 00:06:26,132
Third, artillery firing over open sights.

162
00:06:26,561 --> 00:06:28,559
An army without anti-tank weapons

163
00:06:28,579 --> 00:06:31,256
used its howitzers as anti-tank guns.

164
00:06:31,431 --> 00:06:33,300
Massing the artillery at Chuncheon

165
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:35,885
paid off at the decisive moment.

166
00:06:36,332 --> 00:06:39,449
Fourth, the men who refused to fall back.

167
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:41,718
According to the U.S. Army history,

168
00:06:41,738 --> 00:06:43,823
the North Korean 2nd Division lost

169
00:06:43,843 --> 00:06:45,906
about 40 percent of its strength.

170
00:06:45,965 --> 00:06:47,868
Within days of the invasion,

171
00:06:47,888 --> 00:06:49,587
North Korea had to replace its

172
00:06:49,607 --> 00:06:51,578
corps and division commanders.

173
00:06:52,009 --> 00:06:55,097
Even Shtykov, the Soviet ambassador in Pyongyang,

174
00:06:55,117 --> 00:06:57,611
described the causes in his report:

175
00:06:57,735 --> 00:07:00,196
rugged terrain, and the unexpectedly

176
00:07:00,216 --> 00:07:03,007
courageous defense of the 6th Division.

177
00:07:03,452 --> 00:07:05,139
More important than any number

178
00:07:05,159 --> 00:07:06,826
was the time they bought.

179
00:07:07,241 --> 00:07:09,031
While II Corps was pinned down at

180
00:07:09,051 --> 00:07:11,022
Chuncheon, the plan to encircle

181
00:07:11,042 --> 00:07:13,332
Seoul from behind fell apart.

182
00:07:13,442 --> 00:07:15,289
Much of the South Korean army was able

183
00:07:15,309 --> 00:07:17,313
to escape south of the Han River.

184
00:07:17,743 --> 00:07:19,378
And on that borrowed time,

185
00:07:19,398 --> 00:07:22,518
UN forces began arriving in Korea.

186
00:07:22,578 --> 00:07:24,687
It was the first step toward the Pusan

187
00:07:24,707 --> 00:07:26,796
Perimeter and the Inchon landing.

188
00:07:27,227 --> 00:07:28,771
The 6th Division's fight

189
00:07:28,791 --> 00:07:30,671
did not end at Chuncheon.

190
00:07:30,749 --> 00:07:32,892
A week later at Dongnak-ri, the 7th

191
00:07:32,912 --> 00:07:34,781
Regiment ambushed a North Korean

192
00:07:34,801 --> 00:07:37,228
regiment and dealt it a heavy blow.

193
00:07:37,678 --> 00:07:39,347
Today, a memorial in

194
00:07:39,367 --> 00:07:41,520
Chuncheon honors the battle.

195
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:44,437
Every June, the city commemorates what Koreans

196
00:07:44,457 --> 00:07:47,730
call the 'Great Victory at Chuncheon.'

197
00:07:48,154 --> 00:07:49,334
The Soyang River still

198
00:07:49,354 --> 00:07:51,200
flows just as it did then.

199
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:53,430
On the first day of the war,

200
00:07:53,450 --> 00:07:57,795
as everything collapsed, one division did not.

201
00:07:57,837 --> 00:08:00,105
What they defended was not just a city,

202
00:08:00,125 --> 00:08:02,383
but time for an entire nation.

203
00:08:02,805 --> 00:08:04,405
If this story meant something to you,

204
00:08:04,425 --> 00:08:06,760
please subscribe and like the video.

205
00:08:06,801 --> 00:08:07,931
We'll continue to bring the

206
00:08:07,951 --> 00:08:09,890
forgotten records of war to light.
